.SH OPTIONS
.SS "POSITIONAL\ ARGUMENTS"
.TP
.I FILE
DXF file to browse
.SS "OPTIONAL\ ARGUMENTS"
.TP
.B \-h\fR,\ \fB\-\-help
show a help message and exit
.TP
.B \-l\ \fILINE\fR,\ \fB\-\-line\ \fILINE
go to line number
.TP
.B \-g\ \fIHANDLE\fR,\ \fB\-\-handle\ \fIHANDLE
go to entity by
.IR HANDLE ,
.I HANDLE
has to be a hex value without any prefix like
.RB \(oq fefe \(cq
.SH ENVIRONMENT
See
.BR ezdxf (1).
.SH FILES
See
.BR ezdxf (1).
.P
The browse command stores options in the config file,
e.g. for the Notepad++ on Windows:
.P
.in +7n
.EX
.B [browse\-command]
.B \:
.B text_editor\ =\ "C:\[rs]Program Files\[rs]Notepad++\[rs]notepad++.exe"\ "{filename}"\ \-n{num}
.B icon_size\ =\ 32
.EE
.in
.P
.B text_editor
is a simple format string:
.B text_editor.format(filename="test.dxf",\ num=100)
.P
Quote commands including spaces and always quote the filename argument!
.P
For
.BR xed (1)
on Linux Mint use (note: absolute path to executable):
.P
.in +7n
.EX
.B [browse\-command]
.B \:
.B text_editor\ =\ /usr/bin/xed\ "{filename}"\ +{num}
.B icon_size\ =\ 32
.EE
.in
.P
For
.BR gedit (1)
use (untested):
.P
.in +7n
.EX
.B [browse\-command]
.B \:
.B text_editor\ =\ /usr/bin/gedit\ +{num}\ "{filename}"
.B icon_size\ =\ 32
.EE
.in
.SH EXAMPLES
.P
Browse the internal structure of a DXF file like a file system:
.P
.in +7n
.EX
.B $\ ezdxf\ browse\ gear.dxf
.EE
.in
